> The 64 matrices are used for computation within an 8-level system; they do not serve to generate a geometry. Their utility lies in what they diagonalize: any observable, gate, or Hamiltonian of this qudit can be uniquely expressed in this basis. In quantum computing, an 8-dimensional qudit replaces three qubits, since $8=2^3$. The operators $W_{s,t}$ provide the generalized Pauli gates. They are used to compile a circuit, formulate an error-correcting code for the qudit, and track how an error propagates through discrete phase space. The Heisenberg group—of order 512—classifies these errors, while $\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb{Z}/8\mathbb{Z})$—of order 384—determines which transformations leave the code invariant. $H=\sum_{s,t}c_{s,t}W_{s,t}$, where $c_{s,t}=\frac{1}{8}\operatorname{Tr}(W_{s,t}^\dagger H)$. The coefficients $c_{s,t}$ represent transition frequencies and couplings; they are tuned via microwave or laser pulses applied to an ion, a superconducting circuit, or a multi-level nuclear spin. The clock (Z) and shift (X) operators are the two generators that the experimental setup must be capable of applying. In quantum information, the 64-state phase space serves as a lookup table. A measurement in a basis shifted by $W_{s,t}$ is a $\mathbb{Z}/8\mathbb{Z}$-valued measurement. This approach allows for the construction of key distribution protocols, state tomography, and entanglement tests for pairs of qudits. The case $d=2$ recovers the Pauli matrices and standard qubit protocols; the case $d=8$ follows the same construction but involves more levels.
> 
> Applications—Quantum memory: preserving a logical state longer than physical qubits, once the error rate falls below the threshold.
> Universal fault-tolerant computing: surface code combined with magic-state distillation; the current roadmap for superconducting processors and certain trapped-ion systems.
> Simulation of topological phases: on-chip realization of (e) and (m) anyons, or Majorana modes at wire edges, to test fusion and braiding.
> Metrology and sensors: topological degeneracy and protected edge modes serve as a stable reference, relatively insensitive to local disorder.
> Theory: a discrete laboratory for modular tensor categories, Chern–Simons theories, and the fractional Hall effect.
> 
> Practical limitation: overhead. A logical qubit with sufficient code distance requires hundreds to thousands of physical qubits, and magic-state distillation dominates the cost of useful algorithms.
> 
> Some codes go further. If the anyons are non-Abelian (Ising, Fibonacci, SU(2)k Chern–Simons theories), the braiding of their worldlines acts via a unitary representation on the fusion space. For Fibonacci anyons, these braids are dense in the unitary group: the computation is topological, protected by the energy gap as long as the anyons remain separated. No machine of this type exists yet; Abelian codes (surface code, color code) are the ones currently being built. Applications
